# Engineering quality bar

Write like a careful senior Go engineer on a security-sensitive CLI. Ponytail chooses *how little* to build; this rule sets *how well* what you build must be.

## Quality & readability

- Clear names; small functions; one concern per package/file where practical
- Prefer stdlib and existing `internal/` patterns over new frameworks
- Comments explain *why* (invariants, trust boundaries), not narrate *what*
- Match surrounding style (`gofmt`, existing error wrapping, table-driven tests)

## Maintainability

- Smallest coherent diff; no drive-by refactors unless required
- Update docs/examples when user-facing behavior changes
- Do not leave dead code, unused exports, or duplicate helpers

## Testability

- Behavior changes need tests (table-driven preferred)
- Prefer pure logic under `internal/` that is easy to unit-test
- Use synthetic fixtures only — never realistic secrets (see workflow rule)
- Non-trivial logic is unfinished without a failing-path check (see Ponytail)

## Performance

- Avoid buffering entire plans/reports when streaming already exists
- Respect existing size limits and fail-closed truncation
- No unbounded maps/labels in metrics or logs; watch allocations in hot paths
- Measure before micro-optimizing; prefer correctness + clear bounds

## Security

- Fail closed at trust boundaries (adapters, webhooks, policy I/O, SSRF)
- Do not weaken paths-only attributes, redaction, or policy-as-publish-gate without an explicit product decision + docs
- After substantive first-party code changes, run available Snyk/security checks when configured
- Follow `SECURITY.md` and `.cursor/rules/go-cli.mdc`

## Definition of done (code)

- Compiles; focused tests pass; lints clean for touched packages when tooling is available
- No secrets in diff; README/docs updated if operators need to know
